{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/cdd5f0c58228b47a0bbba77daf2b426ce0247e71259e2b5af1a4a80fa833a036.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b56d8794211aefc41204a7195d42e43699689d92d0a585cd18cb56c0102f7aa"} {"question" "q-161039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b56d8794211aefc41204a7195d42e43699689d92d0a585cd18cb56c0102f7aa"} {"question" "q-161039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "