/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/assets/8c3056de2634d5315aebcbc978bf279eeb95da736ab6796c9d86dc39d9feadc5.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b56d8794211aefc41204a7195d42e43699689d92d0a585cd18cb56c0102f7aa"} {"question" "q-83499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054995/state.json" "file_sha256" "2b56d8794211aefc41204a7195d42e43699689d92d0a585cd18cb56c0102f7aa"} {"question" "q-83499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multiv